 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Adds the computational cost of the discriminator network to each training step. The discriminator typically requires fewer parameters than the generator but adds significant overhead to the backward pass if not carefully sized [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Improves perceptual quality and high-frequency details compared to MSE/L1-only training [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces sharper, more realistic textures; reduces blurriness [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Harder to train; prone to instability; requires careful hyperparameter tuning for loss weights [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** Adds the forward and backward pass of the discriminator network to each training step [S1], [S3]. The discriminator architecture typically uses 14 convolutional layers with $4 \times 4$ filters and LReLU activations [S3].
+*   **Performance:** Improves perceptual quality and high-frequency details compared to MSE/L1-only training [S1], [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces sharper, more realistic textures; reduces smearing and streak artifacts common in single-view reconstruction [S2], [S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful hyperparameter tuning for loss weights; necessitates per-reconstruction discriminator retraining [S3].
 *   **When to use:** When photorealism and high-frequency detail are prioritized over strict pixel-wise accuracy [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) introduced by Goodfellow et al. [S1]. In the context of volumetric rendering and super-resolution, it was employed to train a discriminator network in parallel with a super-resolution generator to classify images as ground truth or predicted, using the discriminator's output as a loss term for the generator [S1].
+Originated from Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) introduced by Goodfellow et al. [S1]. In volumetric rendering and super-resolution, it was first employed to train a discriminator network in parallel with a super-resolution generator to classify images as ground truth or not, using the discriminator's output as a loss term [S1]. The methodology was subsequently adapted for point-based graphics with multi-scale discriminators [S2], fluid reconstruction with fully convolutional discriminators [S3], and hierarchical 3D Gaussian splats with pose-aware contrastive objectives [S4].
 
